# Using ArkType for TypeScript runtime validation
created: 2023-05-22, last updated: 2026-04-23, [Read more](https://blog.logrocket.com/using-arktype-typescript-runtime-validation/)

```
const messageTypes = scope({
info: {
message: "string"
},
error: {
id: "number",
code: "string",
}
}).compile();
const responseScope = scope({
data: "string",
exception: "error",
message: "info"
},
{
imports: [messageTypes]
...
In the example above, we define a scope called `messageTypes`, which contains two types: `info` and `error`.
We then import this scope into the `responseScope`, which references the two type definitions for the `exception` and `message` types.
Scopes can be useful for reusing common types across multiple type definitions.
ArkType also provides a set of inbuil subtypes that can be used within scopes.
These subtypes are inferred as `string`s, but they also provide additional validation at runtime.

